For non-negative integer $K$, a classical MAC will be called \textbf{$(N,K)$-local} if it can be decomposed as
\begin{equation}
\label{Eq:N,K-local-general}
    p(b|a_1\cdots  a_N)=\sum_{\substack{S\\|S|=K}}p_S\sum_{m\in\mathcal{M}_S}[d(b|m)q_S(m|(a_s)_{s\in S})],
\end{equation}
where the outer sum is over all subsets of $\{1,2,\cdots, N\}$ whose cardinality is $K$, and $p_S$ is the probability that the particle is initially prepared in one of the paths belonging to $S$.  The collection of all such MACs will be denoted by $\mathcal{C}_{N,K}(\bm{\mathcal{A}};\mathcal{B})$, or simply $\mathcal{C}_{N,K}$.  Note that $\mathcal{C}_{N}=\mathcal{C}_{N,1}$.

